const s=(id,title,theme,text,transfer)=>({id,title,subtitle:theme,theme,text,targets:[],questions:[],retell:'Retell the situation in your own words. Explain the choice, the reason and what changed. Avoid copying the scene.',transfer:transfer||'Move the situation somewhere else in your life. Use one expression for a specific new idea.'});
export const mountainStories=[
 s('market-decision','A Saturday market','Choices and viewpoints','The council proposed a Saturday market beside the library. “{From my perspective|From my perspective}, it would bring people together,” said Leena. “{I tend to believe that|I tend to believe that} small local events are worth trying.” Sami was less certain. “{I partially agree with|I partially agree with} that idea, but the buses already stop here. {A potential drawback|One potential drawback} is the extra traffic.” Leena suggested {A reasonable alternative|a reasonable alternative}: a smaller market in the courtyard. Both agreed that {A balanced approach|a balanced approach} should consider residents as well as traders.'),
 s('quiet-route','The quieter route','Reasons and respectful disagreement','“{A compelling argument for|A compelling argument for} the new cycle path is the safer school journey,” said Amir. “{The main reason is that|The main reason is that} children would no longer share the narrow road with delivery vans.” Tara answered, “{I respectfully disagree with|I respectfully disagree with} closing the road all day. Shops still need deliveries.” The planner asked everyone to {Be open to feedback|be open to feedback}. {To some extent|To some extent}, both concerns could be addressed by restricting deliveries only during school hours. The final arrangement, Tara admitted, {It largely depends on|largely depends on} how clearly the times are explained.'),
 s('late-club','The club that stayed open','Contrast without contradiction','The club room was small. {On the other hand|On the other hand}, it was free and close to the bus stop. {At the same time|At the same time}, several members needed a quieter space. {Even though|Even though} the room was convenient, the group looked at another option. {Despite|Despite} the higher rent, a shared room nearby seemed possible. The old room suited informal chats, {Whereas|whereas} the new one suited focused work. {Rather than|Rather than} choose immediately, the members tested both for a week.'),
 s('two-walks','Two walks through one neighborhood','Comparison and clarification','{In contrast to|In contrast to} the busy main road, the path beside the river felt calm. The walking group still had to consider safety after dark. “{Taking everything into account|Taking everything into account}, we should use the river path in daylight,” said the guide. “{In other words|In other words}, the route can change with the conditions.” A broken lamp was not proof that the entire path was unsafe, but {This suggests that|this suggests that} maintenance needed attention. “{Put simply|Put simply}, we need a route people can use confidently,” she added.'),
 s('repair-cafe','A repair café begins','Practical solutions','The neighborhood wanted to {Make a meaningful contribution|make a meaningful contribution} to reducing waste. Instead of telling people to buy less, volunteers tried to {Address the root cause|address the root cause} of discarded appliances: many owners could not find affordable repairs. They hoped to {Come up with a practical solution|come up with a practical solution}. A monthly repair café could {Reduce the risk of|reduce the risk of} usable items being thrown away. Safety checks would also {Minimize the negative consequences|minimize the negative consequences} of an inexperienced person attempting a dangerous repair.'),
 s('rain-plan','The event after the rain','Cause, action and progress','A wet forecast could {Pose a serious challenge|pose a serious challenge} for the outdoor fair. The organizers decided to {Adopt a flexible approach|adopt a flexible approach} and prepare an indoor layout as well. Careful preparation can {Lead to|lead to} a calmer event, while confusion may {Result in|result in} wasted work. Everyone could {Contribute to|contribute to} the backup plan by checking one practical detail. Even small actions could {Make a significant difference|make a significant difference}. With regular checks, the team began to {Make steady progress|make steady progress} instead of repeatedly starting again.'),
 s('first-placement','The first placement','Learning by doing','The college arranged a short placement so students could {Acquire practical experience|acquire practical experience}. They would {Apply theoretical knowledge|apply theoretical knowledge} to real requests from visitors. A supervisor designed questions to {Cultivate intellectual curiosity|cultivate intellectual curiosity}, rather than simply supply answers. Pair work would {Encourage active participation|encourage active participation}, and unfamiliar problems could {Strengthen problem-solving skills|strengthen problem-solving skills}. The students hoped to {Develop transferable skills|develop transferable skills} that would remain useful beyond this particular workplace.'),
 s('open-workshop','An open workshop','Opportunities and independence','The community center wanted to {Provide equal opportunities|provide equal opportunities} for residents with different schedules. Evening sessions would {Support independent learning|support independent learning} with equipment people could borrow. The aim was to {Encourage lifelong learning|encourage lifelong learning}, not require a formal qualification. Leena hoped to {Gain hands-on experience|gain hands-on experience} repairing furniture. “I want to {Make the most of|make the most of} these sessions,” she said, “and {Take full advantage of|take full advantage of} the advice available.” Meeting people from other trades could also {Expand my perspective|expand my perspective} on what useful skills look like.'),
 s('shared-display','A display made together','Shared work and responsibility','For the local-history display, the students had to {Work collaboratively|work collaboratively}, not just place separate pages beside each other. They agreed to {Share responsibilities|share responsibilities} and {Resolve conflicts constructively|resolve conflicts constructively}. Keeping promises would help them {Build mutual trust|build mutual trust}. Each person would {Be accountable for|be accountable for} checking the dates on one section. When the printer failed, the team had to {Rise to the challenge|rise to the challenge} and find another way to finish the display without blaming the newest member.'),
 s('pottery-room','The uneven bowl','Experience and persistence','At the pottery workshop, Mira had to {Step outside my comfort zone|step outside her comfort zone}. Her first bowl collapsed, so she began to {Learn through trial and error|learn through trial and error}. A different clay required her to {Adapt to changing circumstances|adapt to changing circumstances} rather than repeat the same movements. She would {Take responsibility for|take responsibility for} cleaning the shared table, even after a frustrating lesson. Finishing a small, uneven bowl helped her {Achieve a sense of accomplishment|achieve a sense of accomplishment}. Progress did not have to look impressive to be real.'),
 s('water-notebook','The water-testing notebook','Evidence and cautious conclusions','Before discussing the stream, the students needed to {Conduct thorough research|conduct thorough research} and {Gather reliable evidence|gather reliable evidence}. They agreed to {Evaluate the evidence|evaluate the evidence} before choosing an explanation. A blocked drain was {A plausible explanation|a plausible explanation}, but blaming it immediately would rest on {An underlying assumption|an underlying assumption} that every unusual result came from that source. Their report should {Identify key limitations|identify key limitations} and {Interpret findings cautiously|interpret findings cautiously}, so readers could see what the samples did and did not show.'),
 s('survey-caution','What the survey cannot prove','Connections and evidence','A survey found that frequent library visitors also reported enjoying reading. That pattern could {Correlate with|correlate with} access to quiet study space, but it did not {Establish a causal relationship|establish a causal relationship}. The team could not honestly {Present compelling evidence|present compelling evidence} that visiting the library caused every improvement. {On the basis of|On the basis of} the available responses, they described the connection carefully. {In light of|In light of} the missing information, {A noteworthy finding|a noteworthy finding} remained a starting point, not a final explanation. They needed {A robust methodology|a robust methodology} and more {Empirical support|empirical support} before they could {Draw a well-supported conclusion|draw a well-supported conclusion}.'),
 s('request-room','A room for a small group','A polite, purposeful request','Leena drafted a message to the center: “{I am writing to inquire about|I am writing to inquire about} a room for a small reading group. {Would it be possible to|Would it be possible to} meet there on Thursday evenings? {I would appreciate it if|I would appreciate it if} you could confirm the capacity. {Could you please clarify|Could you please clarify} whether the room has step-free access? {I would be grateful if|I would be grateful if} you could suggest another room if it is unavailable.” Each question served a specific need rather than making the message merely longer.'),
 s('changed-booking','A changed booking','Following up without pressure','“{I am following up on|I am following up on} our room booking,” wrote Omar. “{Due to unforeseen circumstances|Due to unforeseen circumstances}, we need a smaller space. {I would like to request|I would like to request} a change if one is available. {Please find attached|Please find attached} our revised plan. You can review it {At your convenience|at your convenience}; the event is still three weeks away. {I apologize for any inconvenience|I apologize for any inconvenience} caused by the change.” Omar checked that the attachment was actually included before sending the message.'),
 s('helpful-reply','A reply worth sending','Closing a message naturally','The coordinator offered a short visit to inspect the room. Omar replied, “{I would welcome the opportunity to|I would welcome the opportunity to} see the space before confirming. {I am available to|I am available to} visit on Tuesday afternoon. {I look forward to hearing from you|I look forward to hearing from you} about a suitable time. {Thank you for your consideration|Thank you for your consideration}.” He did not add every polite phrase he knew. The purpose was to make the next step clear, not to display a list of expressions.'),
 s('old-radio','An old radio, a new interest','Reflecting on experience','“{Looking back|Looking back}, {I was initially hesitant|I was initially hesitant} to join the repair group,” Mira said. “{What stood out to me was|What stood out to me was} how patiently everyone explained things. {One thing I appreciate is|One thing I appreciate is} that a beginner can ask simple questions. {I eventually realized that|I eventually realized that} being useful did not mean knowing everything. {It made me realize that|It made me realize that} I could contribute while still learning.” Her example was specific: she had cleaned and labeled the parts of a donated radio.'),
 s('library-thoughts','A conversation on the way home','Natural personal opinions','“{For the most part|For the most part}, I prefer learning with someone else,” said Mira. “{A good example would be|A good example would be} the day we repaired the radio.” Omar preferred quieter work, and she answered, “{I can see why|I can see why} that suits you. {That said|That said}, explaining an idea aloud helps me notice gaps.” When he asked about future plans, she paused: “{One thing that comes to mind|One thing that comes to mind} is a regular repair club. {If I had the opportunity|If I had the opportunity}, I would help organize one.”'),
 s('busy-calendar','The calendar with empty spaces','Habits that can continue','To {Manage my time effectively|manage my time effectively}, I first needed to {Set realistic goals|set realistic goals}. I could not fill every evening with extra work. I chose to {Maintain a consistent routine|maintain a consistent routine} and {Keep track of deadlines|keep track of deadlines} on a small calendar. This helped me {Stay on top of|stay on top of} important commitments. I would {Allocate sufficient time|allocate sufficient time} for travel and {Prioritize urgent tasks|prioritize urgent tasks}, while leaving room for unexpected changes.'),
 s('rest-is-part','The evening left free','Longer-term learning','Mira wanted to {Maintain a healthy balance|maintain a healthy balance} between useful activity and rest. {Over the long term|Over the long term}, a routine had to fit her actual life. She could {Put in consistent effort|put in consistent effort} without doing the same amount every evening. Rest was {beneficial|beneficial} for her concentration. {In the long run|In the long run}, she hoped to {Gain valuable insights|gain valuable insights} from many small experiences. The new routine could {Have a positive / negative impact on|have a positive impact on} her work without taking over every other part of her day.'),
 s('funding-conversation','A modest proposal','Reasoning without invented certainty','The group wanted to {Address an issue|address an issue} with the old equipment, but a replacement had to be {feasible|feasible}. Their proposal needed a {coherent|coherent} explanation, a {reliable|reliable} estimate and a {relevant|relevant} example. A {substantial|substantial} increase in capacity was attractive, but there was a {trade-off|trade-off} between cost and convenience. “{I would argue that|I would argue that} easier access matters more than extra features,” said Mira. They would make the room {accessible|accessible} before buying a larger screen.'),
 s('reading-a-study','Before quoting a study','Evidence and implications','An article gave the group {empirical|empirical} information about similar public spaces. Leena checked for {bias|bias} and asked what one practical {implication|implication} might be. She could write “{Research suggests that|Research suggests that}” only if she had actually read and understood the source. A confident introduction would not turn a weak result into strong evidence. She decided to describe the study’s limits as well as its useful observations. {As a result|As a result}, the proposal became more careful and easier to question.'),
 s('careful-report','A careful report','Evidence and word choice','The first report gave a {nuanced|nuanced} account of the problem rather than blaming one person. A {rigorous|rigorous} check made its evidence more {credible|credible}. Some answers were {ambiguous|ambiguous}, so the team did not pretend there was only one {plausible|plausible} explanation. They needed a {robust|robust} method and more {precise|precise} questions. A useful report could admit uncertainty without becoming vague.'),
 s('two-accounts','Two accounts of the same visit','Separating observation and judgment','An {impartial|impartial} reviewer compared the two accounts. They contained {contradictory|contradictory} details, and the early figures were only {preliminary|preliminary}. The reviewer looked for an {underlying|underlying} difference in how the visits were recorded. A change could be {significant|significant} without being {indicative|indicative} of a lasting trend. She separated an {objective|objective} record of arrival times from a {subjective|subjective} judgment that the visit felt rushed.'),
 s('notice-board','The notice-board investigation','Thinking clearly','A {methodical|methodical} review began with the messages residents had actually received. A {comprehensive|comprehensive} account included both the paper notice and the online update. It was {logical|logical} to compare the two before proposing a fix. The team would {infer|infer} a possible misunderstanding from the replies, but would not {assume|assume} that everyone had read the same notice. A confident statement needed evidence to {substantiate|substantiate} it.'),
 s('learning-circle','The learning circle','Ways to learn together','The new group was {collaborative|collaborative}, but it also supported {autonomous|autonomous} work between meetings. An {engaging|engaging} demonstration led into an {interactive|interactive} activity. Each member offered one {constructive|constructive} comment on a draft. The tutor wanted {contextual|contextual} understanding: learners should know when a phrase fits, not just its definition. The workshop’s {experiential|experiential} approach gave them something concrete to discuss afterward.'),
 s('different-minds','Different minds at the table','Participation and perspective','The design meeting was {inclusive|inclusive}: diagrams, spoken explanations and written notes were all welcome. The {interdisciplinary|interdisciplinary} team combined practical, artistic and {analytical|analytical} skills. An {attentive|attentive} listener noticed a concern the others had missed. A short {reflective|reflective} pause gave everyone time to reconsider the plan. Being {motivated|motivated} did not mean speaking constantly; sometimes the most useful contribution was a careful question.'),
 s('unusual-library','An unusual library idea','Persistence and practical innovation','A {persistent|persistent} volunteer kept looking for a place to store the shared tools. Her {innovative|innovative} idea was a small borrowing cabinet inside an existing shop. The plan had to remain {adaptable|adaptable} as demand changed. It needed an {efficient|efficient} booking process, but a {productive|productive} conversation with the shopkeeper mattered first. A {systematic|systematic} list of safety checks helped the group decide which tools could be borrowed.'),
 s('small-goals','A plan small enough to finish','Work and decisions','A {consistent|consistent} routine made the project feel {manageable|manageable}. The team chose an {attainable|attainable} first goal rather than an impressive promise. A {strategic|strategic} decision was to borrow equipment before buying it. When the deadline changed, the organizer took {decisive|decisive} action, while a {resourceful|resourceful} colleague found another printer. Careful preparation had made quick action possible.'),
 s('detailed-plan','The detailed plan that could change','Planning without rigidity','The organizer was {meticulous|meticulous} about dates and safety checks. A {dependable|dependable} colleague confirmed the deliveries. Their plan was also {pragmatic|pragmatic}: if a cheaper arrangement worked, they would use it. A {flexible|flexible} timetable allowed for late arrivals. The organizer’s {decisiveness|decisiveness} was useful only because she listened to the new information before choosing.'),
 s('shared-heating','A warmer community room','Resources and fairness','The group wanted an {equitable|equitable} arrangement for using the heated room. It had to remain {affordable|affordable} when funds were {scarce|scarce}. A {renewable|renewable} energy option was attractive, but it was not yet practical for this building. There was {widespread|widespread} support for fixing the insulation first. Leaving the room closed all winter would have a {detrimental|detrimental} effect on the groups that met there.'),
 s('new-tools','A new tool, carefully introduced','Change and unintended effects','An {emerging|emerging} booking technology looked promising, but the volunteers checked for {adverse|adverse} effects on people without smartphones. The {pervasive|pervasive} assumption that everyone was online needed questioning. A {gradual|gradual} change would let people learn the system. An {unprecedented|unprecedented} number of requests could still create problems. Clear alternatives could {mitigate|mitigate} confusion rather than {exacerbate|exacerbate} an existing gap.'),
 s('clearer-meeting','A clearer meeting','Useful thinking verbs','A small diagram could {enhance|enhance} the explanation and {facilitate|facilitate} discussion. The chair would {allocate|allocate} time to each question. She asked people to {clarify|clarify} what they meant, {illustrate|illustrate} it with an example and {distinguish|distinguish} a fact from an interpretation. A quiet reply might {imply|imply} uncertainty, but she would ask rather than guess.'),
 s('last-page','Before the final page','Understanding and checking','The notes seemed to {indicate|indicate} a change in preferences, but one interview appeared to {contradict|contradict} that pattern. The team needed to {interpret|interpret} both accounts fairly. They would {analyze|analyze} the replies, {assess|assess} the limits and {synthesize|synthesize} the main ideas. Finally, someone would {summarize|summarize} the findings and {verify|verify} the dates. The report would {emphasize|emphasize} what they had learned without hiding what remained unclear.'),
 s('opinions-at-lunch','Opinions at the lunch table','Different strengths of opinion','“{As far as I’m concerned|As far as I’m concerned}, a useful café needs room for conversation,” said Nila. “{The way I see it|The way I see it}, the music is too loud.” Omar replied, “{I’d say that|I’d say that} the owner wants a lively atmosphere. {It seems to me that|It seems to me that} a quiet hour could suit both groups.” Nila did not need to sound certain about the owner’s intention. Her own preference was clear; the explanation for his choice was only an impression.'),
 s('strong-and-tentative','A claim worth qualifying','Confidence and support','“{I firmly believe that|I firmly believe that} everyone should be able to enter the building safely,” said the volunteer. “{I am convinced that|I am convinced that} the broken handrail needs attention.” On the larger funding question, she was more tentative: “{I am inclined to think that|I am inclined to think that} repairing the existing entrance is better than adding a separate one.” Her confidence changed with the kind of evidence she had, not with the length of the phrase.'),
 s('fair-disagreement','A fair disagreement','Concession and limits','“{I completely agree with|I completely agree with} making the workshop affordable,” said the manager. “{While it is true that|While it is true that} free places would help some residents, we still have material costs.” A volunteer answered, “{To a certain extent|To a certain extent}, donations could cover them. {Having said that|Having said that}, we cannot depend on donations alone.” “{To be fair|To be fair}, neither proposal solves everything,” said Nila. They returned to the costs instead of treating disagreement as a personal insult.'),
 s('uncertain-opening','Is the center open?','Honest uncertainty','“{As far as I know|As far as I know}, the center opens at nine,” said Omar. “{If I remember correctly|If I remember correctly}, the notice mentions a later start this Saturday.” Nila checked her phone. “{I’m not entirely sure|I’m not entirely sure} the online listing has been updated.” “{To be honest|To be honest}, I would rather call than make the whole group wait outside,” she added. Whether they could begin on time, Omar agreed, {It depends on|depends on} getting a clear answer.'),
 s('important-not-loud','An important point, calmly made','Emphasis without exaggeration','“{The key point is that|The key point is that} people need a safe route home,” said the organizer. “{My primary concern is|My primary concern is} the late bus connection.” She wanted to {Draw attention to|draw attention to} a practical problem, not make the speech dramatic. Transport became a {High priority|high priority}, and the final departure time was a {Key factor|key factor} in the plan. “{It is worth considering|It is worth considering} an earlier finish,” a colleague suggested.'),
 s('claims-not-certainty','When emphasis goes too far','Belief, fact and careful wording','“{There is no denying that|There is no denying that} the entrance is narrow,” said the guide, pointing at the doorway. The next statement was less certain: “{It is widely believed that|It is widely believed that} the building once had another entrance.” She explained that local belief was not proof of the building’s history. “{What I want to emphasize is|What I want to emphasize is} the difference between what we can observe and what we still need to check.” Visitors could question the story without disputing the visible doorway.'),
 s('two-good-reasons','Two reasons, not ten connectors','Adding support','The library proposal offered more than extra shelves. {In addition to|In addition to} lending tools, it could host short demonstrations. “The room is accessible. {Furthermore|Furthermore}, it is beside the main bus stop,” the coordinator wrote in a formal proposal. Another member added, “The existing staff can manage the small pilot. {Moreover|Moreover}, we already have secure storage.” In the spoken meeting, they used simpler wording: the project could provide {Not only ... but also|not only equipment but also advice}. Each addition supplied a new reason.'),
 s('comparing-schedules','Two timetables side by side','Making a fair comparison','The morning group needed quiet work; the evening group needed quick access after work. {Similarly|Similarly}, both needed a room they could reach without stairs. {Unlike|Unlike} the old timetable, the revised one left space between bookings. {In comparison with|In comparison with} the first plan, it was less crowded. {By comparison|By comparison}, the weekend arrangement was almost unchanged. The team compared the same features each time, {As opposed to|as opposed to} choosing whichever detail made one plan look best.'),
 s('two-small-projects','What can travel between projects?','Comparing a process','The garden team tested one small area before changing the whole space. {In the same way|In the same way}, the library could test a short borrowing period before expanding it. The projects were different, but the process was comparable. {Despite the fact that|Despite the fact that} the library had more visitors, it could still begin with a limited trial. The rules would apply {Regardless of|regardless of} who borrowed the tools. Fairness meant explaining the same conditions to everyone.'),
 s('correcting-a-claim','Correcting the wrong impression','Contrast and direct correction','“A trial means the group has no confidence in the idea,” said a visitor. “{On the contrary|On the contrary}, it shows that we are willing to check whether it works,” replied the coordinator. She was correcting a claim, not merely introducing another topic. {In this context|In this context}, a trial was a way to learn. {More specifically|More specifically}, the team wanted to understand waiting times, {In particular|in particular} on busy evenings. Capacity mattered {Especially when|especially when} several people requested the same tool. {In terms of|In terms of} safety, the rules would be the same from the start.'),
 s('examples-that-help','An example that actually explains','Illustration and precision','“Some tools are difficult for beginners,” said the trainer. “{For example|For example}, this heavy drill requires more control.” She chose another object: “{For instance|For instance}, a small hand tool is easier to demonstrate.” {To illustrate|To illustrate} the difference, she let visitors compare the handles. She began with simple tools {Such as|such as} pliers and screwdrivers. The visitors needed to see the distinction, not hear several introductory phrases in one sentence.'),
 s('a-clear-case','A clear case from the garden','Choosing and explaining an example','The raised planter was {A case in point|a case in point}: a small change had made the garden easier to use. “{One notable example is|One notable example is} the lower section for seated gardeners,” said the guide. {As an illustration|As an illustration}, she described a visitor who could now reach the herbs. The path was shared, {That is to say|that is to say}, nobody needed a separate entrance. “{To put it simply|To put it simply}, the design lets more people take part.”'),
 s('source-on-the-table','Put the source on the table','Attributing evidence honestly','“{According to|According to} the maintenance log, the lamp failed last month,” said Nila. “{Based on|Based on} those dates, we should ask whether the reports reached the repair team.” The log could {Support a claim|support a claim} about a delay, but they still needed to {Evaluate evidence|evaluate evidence} from other records. A neat chart was not automatically {Compelling evidence|compelling evidence}. Someone had to check how the information was collected.'),
 s('what-research-means','What does the evidence allow?','Language for actual evidence','The tutor wrote three possible openings on the board: “{Evidence indicates that|Evidence indicates that},” “{Studies have shown that|Studies have shown that},” and “I think.” The first two required actual evidence; the last clearly marked a personal position. {In practice|In practice}, writers sometimes confused those roles. “{This is evident in|This is evident in} a draft that mentions research but names no finding,” she explained. The class practiced distinguishing a sourced claim from an observation instead of inventing a result to sound authoritative.'),
 s('garden-labels','Labels beside the pond','Defining and describing','The guide explained that “habitat” {Refers to|refers to} an organism’s natural home. A wetland {Is defined as|is defined as} land with water conditions that shape its living community. The small display {Consists of|consists of} a map, a photograph and a model. Its frame {Is composed of|is composed of} reused wood. The garden {Is characterized by|is characterized by} several shallow pools. Definitions explained what something meant; descriptions showed what this particular place was like.'),
 s('missed-connection','Why the connection was missed','Naming a cause','The group missed the bus {Because of|because of} a changed meeting point. {Due to|Due to} the old notice, two members waited at the wrong entrance. {Owing to|Owing to} road works, the bus could not stop near them. The confusion seemed to {Stem from|stem from} two announcements that had not been updated together. Part of the delay could {Be attributed to|be attributed to} the road works, but the group still needed to correct its own instructions.'),
 s('one-change-many-effects','One small change, several effects','Outcomes and consequences','A clearer entrance sign could {Bring about|bring about} a useful change in how visitors moved through the building. An unclear sign might instead {Give rise to|give rise to} new confusion. {Consequently|Consequently}, the team tested the wording with actual visitors. They wanted to understand whether a small decision could {Have far-reaching consequences|have far-reaching consequences} for access. The trial revealed a problem; {Therefore|therefore}, the final sign was moved. “{That’s why I think|That’s why I think} a short trial was worth the effort,” said Nila.'),
 s('linked-not-caused','A link is not automatically a cause','Influence and relationships','Opening later could {Have a significant impact on|have a significant impact on} evening attendance. Yet a {Significant impact|significant impact} had to be demonstrated, not assumed. Weather might also {Have an effect on|have an effect on} visits. Longer opening hours could {Be associated with|be associated with} higher attendance without being its only cause. Demand {Is closely related to|is closely related to} the needs of local users. The team would need to {Establish a connection|establish a connection} carefully before explaining the result.'),
 s('before-after-finally','Before, afterward, eventually','Putting events in order','{Prior to|Prior to} the first workshop, the volunteers checked the room. {Initially|Initially}, they planned to use long tables. {Subsequently|Subsequently}, they moved to smaller circles after noticing that people could not hear each other. {Ultimately|Ultimately}, they chose the layout that helped everyone participate. The change had possible {Long-term benefits|long-term benefits}, but they would still review it {From time to time|from time to time}. A useful arrangement should not become untouchable simply because it worked once.'),
 s('unexpected-room','The room that was unavailable','Facing a problem without panic','A lost booking could {Pose a challenge|pose a challenge} for the reading group. The members would {Face difficulties|face difficulties}, but they could still {Deal with|deal with} one issue at a time. They had {Run into a problem|run into a problem} before and found another room. The lack of step-free space continued to {Remain a major concern|remain a major concern}. It was a {Growing concern|growing concern} across several groups, especially when {Limited resources|limited resources} made permanent changes slow.'),
 s('fixing-the-system','Fix the process, not just today','Solutions at different depths','The center needed to {Address a problem|address a problem} with repeated double bookings. Staff decided to {Tackle an issue|tackle an issue} that everyone had noticed but nobody owned. A shared calendar might {Offer a practical solution|offer a practical solution}. To {Come up with a solution|come up with a solution}, they compared actual mistakes. An {Effective solution|effective solution} would not merely move one meeting. It would {Solve the underlying problem|solve the underlying problem}: two staff members confirming the same room separately.'),
 s('safer-route','A route people can follow','Planning and prevention','The coordinator asked the team to {Develop a strategy|develop a strategy} for busy evenings. They would {Implement measures|implement measures} such as clear arrows and a checked closing routine. They also needed to {Take steps to|take steps to} keep the ramp clear. A wrongly placed table could {Prevent someone from|prevent someone from} entering independently. The aim was to make access reliable, not require every visitor to ask for help.'),
 s('a-shared-decision','The decision everyone can explain','Agreement and evaluation','A larger room would {Require careful consideration|require careful consideration}. Before they could {Reach a decision|reach a decision}, the members had to {Explore possibilities|explore possibilities}. They would not {Reach a consensus|reach a consensus} simply by avoiding disagreement. A {Reasonable assumption|reasonable assumption} was that evening users needed a late bus, but this could {Raise a question|raise a question} about the last departure. The final plan should {Meet the needs of|meet the needs of} those users rather than merely look convenient on paper.'),
 s('a-small-investigation','A question small enough to investigate','Research as a process','The class would {Conduct research|conduct research} on how visitors found the right entrance. The {Research project|research project} began with one question, not a preferred answer. Students would {Collect data|collect data} and {Conduct an experiment|conduct an experiment} comparing two sign layouts. They could {Propose a hypothesis|propose a hypothesis} about clearer arrows, then {Test a theory|test a theory} using observations. They would {Analyze findings|analyze findings} before they could {Draw a conclusion|draw a conclusion} or {Gain insight into|gain insight into} the visitors’ choices.'),
 s('a-first-achievement','Progress before perfection','Achievement through practice','Mira wanted to {Pursue a goal|pursue a goal} she could explain: completing a useful repair independently. Her {Practical experience|practical experience} grew slowly, but she could {Make progress|make progress} each week. A clear explanation let her {Demonstrate an understanding of|demonstrate an understanding of} the safety steps. {Critical thinking|Critical thinking} mattered when an instruction did not fit the situation. The improvement might not change her {Academic performance|academic performance} directly, but it was still a {Positive outcome|positive outcome}. Small changes could eventually become a {Substantial improvement|substantial improvement} and {Make a big difference|make a big difference} to her confidence.'),
 s('more-than-a-qualification','More than a qualification','Education and opportunity','A community program hoped to {Improve access to|improve access to} practical learning. Residents would have {Immediate access|immediate access} to advice during the weekly drop-in. Some hoped to {Apply for a scholarship|apply for a scholarship}, {Earn a degree|earn a degree} or {Pursue higher education|pursue higher education}. Others simply wanted {Meaningful interaction|meaningful interaction} and useful skills. Their {Diverse perspectives|diverse perspectives} challenged a {Common misconception|common misconception}: learning matters only when it leads to a formal qualification. The repair project also connected those skills with {Sustainable development|sustainable development}.'),
 s('a-new-semester','The first week of a course','Campus routines as everyday situations','Before the term began, Omar had to {Register for classes|register for classes} and check the {Course requirements|course requirements}. He planned to {Attend a lecture|attend a lecture}, {Take notes|take notes} and ask questions during {Office hours|office hours}. To {Meet a deadline|meet a deadline}, he needed to {Submit an assignment|submit an assignment} before leaving for a family visit. His goal was to {Complete a course|complete a course} without pretending that he could do all the work in one night.'),
 s('catching-up','Catching up without giving up','Managing missed work','A short illness caused Omar to {Fall behind|fall behind}. He needed to {Catch up on|catch up on} missed reading and {Keep track of|keep track of} revised dates. His tutor asked him to {Seek clarification|seek clarification} whenever an instruction was unclear. During the next class he could {Participate in a discussion|participate in a discussion} rather than wait until he knew everything. His teammates would {Provide feedback|provide feedback} as they began to {Work on a group project|work on a group project}.'),
 s('one-polite-question','A question with a purpose','Requesting and responding','“{With regard to|With regard to} the room booking, {I was wondering whether|I was wondering whether} the entrance will be open before nine,” wrote Nila. The coordinator answered, “{Thank you for bringing this to my attention|Thank you for bringing this to my attention}. I will check with the caretaker.” Nila could {Follow up on|follow up on} the request if no answer arrived, but she would first allow a reasonable amount of time. Politeness did not require hiding the practical question.'),
 s('the-attachment','Before pressing Send','Helpful closing language','Nila checked the message one last time. “{I have attached|I have attached} the list of equipment we need. {Please let me know if|Please let me know if} anything is missing. A reply {At your earliest convenience|at your earliest convenience} would help us arrange the collection. {Thank you for taking the time to|Thank you for taking the time to} review the plan.” She used the request only because the timing genuinely mattered. A closing phrase should support the message rather than create unnecessary pressure.'),
 s('learning-a-place','Learning a place, not a script','Sharing personal experience','“{In my experience|In my experience}, a regular small activity makes it easier to meet people,” Mira said. “{One thing I appreciate about|One thing I appreciate about} this group is that beginners can contribute.” It took time to {Get used to|get used to} unfamiliar tools and to {Figure out|figure out} which questions to ask. She could explain one concrete experience without turning it into a universal rule about everyone.'),
 s('a-walk-through-the-plan','A plan explained aloud','Guiding a listener','“{Let’s look at|Let’s look at} the entrance first,” said the guide. “{Moving on to|Moving on to} the main room, notice the clear space beside the door.” She paused so visitors could see the arrangement. “{The main takeaway is|The main takeaway is} that everyone should be able to reach a seat without asking other people to move.” At the end she added, “{To sum up|To sum up}, the plan works only if we keep those routes clear.” The signposts helped listeners follow the explanation.'),
 s('room-capacity','Can the room hold everyone?','Resources and changing conditions','The team had to {adapt|adapt} when the hall was unavailable. The smaller room had {adequate|adequate} seating, but its {capacity|capacity} was limited. The problem became {apparent|apparent} when the final bookings arrived. Their plan would {consist|consist} of two short sessions rather than one crowded meeting. Volunteers would {distribute|distribute} the materials between the sessions. A {subsequent|subsequent} check would show whether they could {maintain|maintain} a comfortable working {environment|environment} for both groups. One possible {consequence|consequence} was a longer afternoon for the organizers.'),
 s('where-energy-comes-from','Where the garden’s energy comes from','Observation in a living space','The guide asked visitors where the young plants {derive|derive} their energy from. A child pointed toward the sunlight. The group waited for a clearer pattern to {emerge|emerge}: plants in the shaded corner were growing differently. One person began to {attribute|attribute} the difference to water alone. The guide suggested another {approach|approach}: compare the light and water separately. Growth could {fluctuate|fluctuate}, so a single measurement was not a strong basis for a conclusion. They needed a {valid|valid} comparison and a clear {criterion|criterion} for judging the result.'),
 s('booking-details','The booking details','Practical arrangements','Nila made an {appointment|appointment} to inspect the room and checked its {availability|availability}. A written {confirmation|confirmation} would help prevent another lost {reservation|reservation}. The room was part of a public {facility|facility}, but not every group was {eligible|eligible} for a reduced rate. The coordinator explained one important {guideline|guideline}: exits must stay clear. Attendance at a safety briefing was {mandatory|mandatory} for the lead organizer. Nila could now explain the conditions to the rest of the group.'),
 s('cancellation-notice','A notice nobody should miss','Changes and required action','A sudden {cancellation|cancellation} forced the team to {postpone|postpone} the workshop. The coordinator would {notify|notify} everyone and explain the {refund|refund} arrangement. The {revised|revised} date also required a different {venue|venue}. One parking {restriction|restriction} remained in place near the new entrance. {In response to|In response to} several questions, she added a map. Clear information about what changed was more useful than a long explanation of how disappointing the change had been.'),
 s('a-phone-call','A phone call that saves a journey','Natural conversational words','“Is the room {available|available} on Friday?” Nila asked. “{Actually|Actually}, Thursday is easier,” the caretaker replied. “{Apparently|Apparently}, a delivery is expected on Friday, but I need to {confirm|confirm} the time.” “Would Thursday afternoon be {convenient|convenient}?” she asked. “{Definitely|Definitely}. Come at two.” “Two {exactly|exactly}?” “Yes. I can show you the room then, {instead|instead} of making you wait outside.” The brief exchange settled a practical detail without sounding formal or rehearsed.'),
 s('another-small-change','One more small change','Polite conversation','“I will {probably|probably} arrive a little early,” said Omar. “Could you {remind|remind} me which entrance to use?” Nila checked the message. “We need to {reschedule|reschedule} the visit. I am {sorry|sorry}; the caretaker has another appointment.” “Are you {sure|sure}?” he asked. “Yes. {Unfortunately|Unfortunately}, nobody else can open the room.” She added, “{would you mind|Would you mind} meeting on Monday instead?” The request allowed Omar to say whether the change worked for him.'),
 s('advising-meeting','A useful advising meeting','Managing a course','Omar asked his {advisor|advisor} about a timetable {conflict|conflict}. His {assignment|assignment} was due just before a family visit, so he wanted a realistic {arrangement|arrangement}. An {extension|extension} might help, but he needed {permission|permission}, not an assumption. The tutor explained the final {deadline|deadline}, the course {credit|credit} and a {prerequisite|prerequisite} for the next class. A clear answer was more useful than promising to finish everything immediately.'),
 s('course-change','Changing one course','Workload and practical choices','A different class required a small {fee|fee}, and Omar needed a suitable {replacement|replacement} for the time slot he would miss. The office hoped to {resolve|resolve} the issue before the new {semester|semester}. A course {transfer|transfer} was possible if he completed the paperwork. The decision also depended on his {workload|workload}. Changing one class would not help if the new timetable created even more pressure elsewhere.'),
 s('hall-entrance','At the hall entrance','Notices and shared spaces','The notice announced a {temporary|temporary} {closure|closure} of the main {auditorium|auditorium}. Visitors should use the {designated|designated} entrance at the rear. A new {location|location} was listed for the opening talk, which would {commence|commence} at ten. {Attendance|Attendance} at that talk was optional, but a safety session was a {requirement|requirement} for people operating equipment. One clear {update|update} could prevent several different misunderstandings.'),
 s('joining-the-session','Joining a session','Conditions and exceptions','Workshop {enrollment|enrollment} would remain open until Friday. The final form was {due|due} at noon. {Participation|Participation} did not require previous experience, but everyone had to follow the safety instructions. The coordinator could {suspend|suspend} an activity if those instructions were ignored. An {exception|exception} to the equipment rule required a separate review; being an experienced visitor did not automatically remove the condition.'),
 s('a-process-explained','A process, explained step by step','Understanding an explanation','The guide used a small model to {demonstrate|demonstrate} the filtration {process|process}. A diagram showed the {sequence|sequence} of steps. It helped visitors understand a difficult {concept|concept} without knowing technical terms first. Her {emphasis|emphasis} was on what each stage did. A {conventional|conventional} model had one {limitation|limitation}: it could not show every feature of the real stream. The guide made that clear rather than let the model create a misleading {assumption|assumption}.'),
 s('one-pattern-two-explanations','One pattern, two explanations','Thinking about relationships','The class noticed a {correlation|correlation} between sunlight and plant growth, but that pattern did not explain the whole {phenomenon|phenomenon}. They needed to {differentiate|differentiate} two {distinct|distinct} possibilities: more light might help directly, or the sunnier corner might also be warmer. A possible {mechanism|mechanism} had to explain how the change happened. Naming a relationship was not the same as showing its cause.'),
 s('permission-with-conditions','Permission with conditions','Joining ideas precisely','The team could use the courtyard {provided that|provided that} they kept the path clear. They could not begin {unless|unless} the caretaker confirmed access. {Although|Although} the weather looked good, they kept an indoor option. They did not know {whether|whether} every visitor could use the garden entrance. {However|However}, the side door offered another route. They checked the map {in order to|in order to} avoid sending people to the wrong place, {because|because} an unclear direction could delay the whole group.'),
 s('a-beginner-can-help','A beginner can still help','Ability, interest and responsibility','Mira was {interested in|interested in} repairing radios, but she did not claim to know every part. She was {responsible for|responsible for} labeling the pieces she removed. The tutor asked whether she was {capable of|capable of} explaining the safety check in her own words. Doing that mattered more than repeating a phrase she did not understand. With help, she could describe each step clearly and notice where she needed another explanation.'),
 s('a-considerate-request','A considerate request','Asking without hiding the purpose','Nila wanted to {inquire|inquire} about the booking and {request|request} a short visit. She needed {clarification|clarification} about the entrance, not a favor nobody could identify. “I would {appreciate|appreciate} your advice,” she wrote. “Thank you for your {consideration|consideration}.” The coordinator replied that a visit could be arranged at her {convenience|convenience}. A polite message still needed enough detail for someone to answer it.'),
 s('the-follow-up','A useful follow-up','Respectful contact','Omar sent a short {follow-up|follow-up} after receiving no reply. He did not assume the silence was intentional. He explained the possible {inconvenience|inconvenience} and offered to {propose|propose} a different date. The message included the original question, so the coordinator would not have to search for it. When she replied, he confirmed the arrangement and stopped sending reminders. Good follow-up helps complete a task rather than create pressure for its own sake.'),
 s('choosing-what-matters','Choosing what matters','Reasons rather than impressive wording','The cheaper room had one clear {advantage|advantage}: the group could afford to meet regularly. A possible {alternative|alternative} was sharing a larger room with another club. The {drawback|drawback} was reduced control over the schedule. Nila asked everyone to {consider|consider} the practical {benefit|benefit} of each option. A strong personal {perspective|perspective} was welcome, but a person should not {assert|assert} a claim without explaining it. Their main {concern|concern} was access, and that became the first {priority|priority}. They needed {evidence|evidence} to {justify|justify} the extra cost.'),
 s('ready-for-visitors','Ready for visitors','Useful everyday vocabulary','The {department|department} confirmed the room, and the volunteers checked the {equipment|equipment}. They wanted visitors to feel {comfortable|comfortable} asking questions. Clear {information|information} about {registration|registration}, the {schedule|schedule} and {transportation|transportation} was placed on one page. A volunteer could {recommend|recommend} a suitable session without making everyone read the whole program. The {workshop|workshop} felt simpler because the practical details were easier to find.'),
 s('the-first-independent-repair','The first independent repair','Personal growth without exaggeration','The repair was a useful {challenge|challenge}. Mira wanted to {accomplish|accomplish} one task safely, not prove that she knew everything. She could {collaborate|collaborate} with a tutor at first and then work {independently|independently}. Finishing made her more {confident|confident}. The day was {enjoyable|enjoyable} and {memorable|memorable}, but its value was also {practical|practical}. She began to {prefer|prefer} careful explanations to fast demonstrations. Knowing when to ask a question became a {strength|strength}.'),
 s('the-stream-beside-the-garden','The stream beside the garden','Nature and protection','The stream supported local {biodiversity|biodiversity}. Its {ecosystem|ecosystem} included insects, plants and the surrounding soil. The group learned that {conservation|conservation} involved protecting the whole {habitat|habitat}, not just one attractive species. Signs of {contamination|contamination} near a drain needed attention. Farther away, {deforestation|deforestation} could worsen soil loss, while heavy rain increased {erosion|erosion}. The volunteers discussed these connections without claiming that one afternoon’s visit had answered every question.'),
 s('a-resilient-garden','A garden that can recover','Systems and change','A varied planting plan could improve the garden’s {resilience|resilience} after dry weather. The team watched one {variable|variable} at a time rather than change every condition together. Their deliveries also produced {emissions|emissions}, so combining several small journeys was worth considering. The point was to make a useful improvement they could maintain, not pretend that the project had no environmental cost.'),
 s('a-booking-tool','A booking tool people can use','Technology and its limits','The proposed {algorithm|algorithm} sorted requests by the time they arrived. Some {automation|automation} could improve {efficiency|efficiency}, but only if the {dataset|dataset} was accurate. The team checked the {accuracy|accuracy} of several entries before trusting the output. A simple {interface|interface} mattered to the users. Protecting {privacy|privacy} mattered too. A {scalable|scalable} system was useful only if it continued to work as requests increased. Careful {validation|validation} helped reveal mistakes and communicate {uncertainty|uncertainty}.'),
 s('a-neighborhood-budget','A neighborhood budget','Public choices and fairness','The council discussed {affordability|affordability} before deciding on a new service. The {allocation|allocation} of funds should consider local needs, not just the loudest request. A {demographic|demographic} change meant more older residents needed reliable routes. Poor {infrastructure|infrastructure} could increase {inequality|inequality} in access to work and services. A small {subsidy|subsidy} was one option, while a better timetable might improve {productivity|productivity} by reducing wasted travel time.'),
 s('a-policy-that-can-be-explained','A policy people can understand','Incentives and change','The new {policy|policy} offered an {incentive|incentive} for repairing equipment instead of replacing it. A safety {regulation|regulation} still applied to every repair. Fast {urbanization|urbanization} had made shared spaces more valuable, and the council wanted a practical response. Explaining the purpose of a rule could help people follow it, but the rule also needed to be workable in everyday conditions.'),
 s('recognize-recall-use','From a familiar word to a useful word','Learning and memory','A striking picture drew Mira’s {attention|attention}, but attention alone did not establish {comprehension|comprehension}. She needed to explain the meaning. A new {stimulus|stimulus}, such as a different situation, could prompt another attempt at recall. The tutor used {feedback|feedback} and later {reinforcement|reinforcement} without claiming that repetition guaranteed {fluency|fluency}. Useful {engagement|engagement} involved choices and explanations, not only clicks. {Memorization|Memorization} could support learning, while practice in varied situations offered evidence about {retention|retention}. Their {perception|perception} of progress had to match what they could actually do. {Cognition|Cognition} covered more than recognizing an answer on a page.'),
 s('the-short-explanation','The short explanation that worked','Precise descriptions','Nila wanted an {accurate|accurate} description, not a dramatic one. A {concise|concise} note would be {appropriate|appropriate} for the entrance notice. The group was {aware|aware} that visitors would read it quickly. A {convincing|convincing} explanation mattered, but so did an {effective|effective} arrangement behind the words. Clear directions were {crucial|crucial} when a visitor was already late. The test group’s {diverse|diverse} experiences helped reveal problems that one writer had missed.'),
 s('what-the-visitor-needs','What the visitor actually needs','Useful rather than ornamental language','A readable map was {essential|essential} for people using the side entrance. The need became {evident|evident} when several visitors asked the same question. {Frequent|Frequent} changes made the notice confusing, and another revision was {likely|likely} unless the team checked the whole process. It was {necessary|necessary} to agree on one route. A {specific|specific} example helped explain the problem, and the next version produced a {noticeable|noticeable} improvement in how easily people found the room.'),
 s('a-sensible-first-goal','A first goal people can reach','Practical judgments','The organizer suggested a {realistic|realistic} first goal: one reliable session each month. It was a {reasonable|reasonable} request for a small group. A {suitable|suitable} room was more important than an impressive one. Feedback from users was {useful|useful}, and their time was {valuable|valuable}. The borrowing system also needed to be {compatible|compatible} with the way the center already worked. A {balanced|balanced} decision considered those needs together.'),
 s('who-checks-the-checklist','Who checks the checklist?','Responsibility and care','Each volunteer was {accountable|accountable} for a short list of tasks. They were {cautious|cautious} about promising more than they could deliver. Choosing {durable|durable} labels was a {deliberate|deliberate} decision because the equipment would be handled often. {Explicit|Explicit} instructions reduced uncertainty. The coordinator avoided putting {excessive|excessive} pressure on the newest member. A {fair|fair} arrangement gave people a real chance to understand what they had agreed to do.'),
 s('asking-without-pretending','Asking without pretending','Honest attitudes','Mira had a {genuine|genuine} interest in the work, but she was still {hesitant|hesitant} to ask a basic question. Some mistakes were {inevitable|inevitable} while she learned, the tutor explained. Their {initial|initial} plan included time for practice. An {integrated|integrated} approach connected explanation, action and feedback. {Mutual|Mutual} respect made it easier to admit uncertainty. An {occasional|occasional} correction did not mean that the whole effort had failed.'),
 s('a-pilot-before-a-promise','A pilot before a permanent promise','Possibility and proportion','The room change was a trial, not yet a {permanent|permanent} decision. Its {potential|potential} benefits were clear, but actual use still needed checking. Effort should be {proportional|proportional} to the problem being solved. A {sensible|sensible} review would compare the same conditions. The process should be {transparent|transparent}, especially if an {urgent|urgent} repair affected several groups. A {viable|viable} option had to work for the people using the space, not merely fit a diagram.'),
 s('a-choice-to-help','A choice to help','Care, choice and preparation','The project relied on {voluntary|voluntary} help. New members should not feel forced to accept every task. Some visitors were more {vulnerable|vulnerable} to a sudden change in access, so the team would {anticipate|anticipate} their questions. The organizer needed to {acknowledge|acknowledge} concerns and {accommodate|accommodate} different needs. Members could {advocate|advocate} an improvement without speaking on behalf of everyone. A useful {compromise|compromise} would leave each side able to explain what it gained and what it adjusted.'),
 s('another-way-to-help','Another way to help','Organizing action','Sharing equipment could {conserve|conserve} resources, but someone needed to {coordinate|coordinate} the collections. Nila could {cope|cope} with one responsibility well; she would {decline|decline} a second one if she had no time. A short briefing would {enable|enable} new volunteers to work safely and {encourage|encourage} them to ask questions. Before confirming the plan, she would {ensure|ensure} that someone could open the store. A clear agreement would {establish|establish} who did what.'),
 s('test-a-small-change','Test a small change first','Action and evaluation','The group would {evaluate|evaluate} the small trial before trying to {expand|expand} it. A demonstration could {expose|expose} visitors to a useful skill, but it should not rush them. Staff would {implement|implement} one change, then check whether it could {improve|improve} access. The review would {involve|involve} actual users. If people still missed the entrance, the group would {investigate|investigate} why. A new sign was a proposed solution, not proof that the problem had disappeared.'),
 s('keeping-a-local-story','Keeping a local story alive','Participating and preserving','The team wanted to {overcome|overcome} a language barrier without changing the residents’ accounts. People could {participate|participate} through a short conversation, a written note or a photograph. A clear purpose helped {persuade|persuade} some residents to join. The display aimed to {preserve|preserve} useful memories. After each interview, the students would {reflect|reflect} on what needed checking. A later visit could {reinforce|reinforce} their understanding. They could not {rely|rely} on memory alone when someone supplied a date or name.'),
 s('the-plan-after-feedback','The plan after feedback','Improvement as a process','The revised event would {require|require} less equipment. The coordinator could {respond|respond} to the concerns without abandoning the useful parts. The team would {retain|retain} the clear instructions, {revise|revise} the timetable and {seek|seek} advice about the entrance. That change marked a {shift|shift} in attention from adding features to removing barriers. It could {strengthen|strengthen} the relationship with local groups and {support|support} their work over time. They needed an arrangement they could {sustain|sustain}.'),
 s('a-result-worth-noticing','A result worth noticing','Needs and achievement','Better {access|access} to the room was an {achievement|achievement}, but cost remained one important {aspect|aspect} of the decision. The old entrance had been a {barrier|barrier} for some visitors. The group’s {commitment|commitment} continued after the opening event. Limited money was still a {constraint|constraint}, so every {contribution|contribution} had to be considered carefully. Growing {demand|demand} for sessions would bring another set of choices.'),
 s('what-changed-and-why','What changed, and why?','Results, understanding and interaction','There was a {distinction|distinction} between opening a new room and making it useful. The real {impact|impact} depended on what people could do there. A local {initiative|initiative} had provided the chance, while conversations gave {insight|insight} into what residents needed. Regular {interaction|interaction} made the arrangement easier to improve. One positive {outcome|outcome} was a group that kept meeting without a coordinator doing everything. The space had become an {opportunity|opportunity} for people to contribute.'),
 s('after-a-setback','After a setback','Responsibility without self-blame','Mira accepted {responsibility|responsibility} for forgetting to check the booking. It was a {setback|setback}, not the end of the group. Her next {strategy|strategy} was to confirm the room before sending invitations. She noticed a {tendency|tendency} to rush when she felt under pressure. A manageable routine could protect her {well-being|well-being} as well as the project. She could {learn from a setback|learn from a setback} without treating one mistake as a complete description of her ability.'),
 s('a-conscious-change','A conscious change in habit','From advice to action','Mira wanted to {make a conscious effort|make a conscious effort} to listen before proposing a fix. At the next meeting, she could {put into practice|put that advice into practice}. She would {take the initiative|take the initiative} when a useful task needed an owner, but not take over everyone’s work. A {a sense of belonging|sense of belonging} grew when people could contribute in different ways. To {meet expectations|meet expectations}, they first needed to know what the expectations were.'),
 s('experience-with-boundaries','Experience with boundaries','Using a skill without overcommitting','Nila could {draw on experience|draw on experience} from earlier projects without assuming every new group was the same. A friend helped her {keep things in perspective|keep things in perspective} when a session went badly. One kind response could {make a lasting impression|make a lasting impression}, but she did not need to solve every problem herself. She would {set clear boundaries|set clear boundaries} around her available time. Being helpful should not mean making promises she could not keep.'),
 s('conditions-for-a-good-choice','Conditions for a good choice','Willingness, caution and consistency','The team should {be willing to|be willing to} reconsider a decision when the conditions changed. Nila would {be reluctant to|be reluctant to} promise a date before checking the room. Under pressure, anyone could {be prone to|be prone to} overlooking a detail. The final arrangement should {be consistent with|be consistent with} the rules already explained to members. Visitors needed to {be aware of|be aware of} changes early enough to make their own plans.'),
 s('the-choice-has-consequences','The choice has consequences','Thinking beyond the first benefit','The group considered {a practical implication|a practical implication} of closing earlier: some working members would miss most of the session. A rotating timetable might be {a reasonable compromise|a reasonable compromise}. They also looked for {an unintended consequence|an unintended consequence} of moving online. A person without a reliable connection might lose access entirely. A decision could be well intended and still need revision. The group’s job was to notice what actually happened and respond to it.'),
];
